<main id="top" class="subpage-main">
    <section class="page-header"><div class="container">
        <h1>Grounding and bonding in your home</h1>
        <p>Did an inspection flag a problem? Are you adding equipment or replacing a panel? We can check the connections and explain what needs attention.</p>
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/get-help.html">Tell us what you need checked →</a>
    </div></section>
    <section class="content-section"><div class="container"><div class="content-wrapper">
        <h2>What do grounding and bonding do?</h2>
        <p>They do different jobs. Both matter, but a ground rod alone won't make faulty wiring safe.</p>
        <div class="content-columns">
            <div><h3>Grounding connects the system to earth</h3><p>Ground rods and other grounding electrodes help limit voltage from lightning and other electrical disturbances. They don't replace the wiring path that lets a breaker disconnect a fault.</p></div>
            <div><h3>Bonding connects metal parts</h3><p>Bonding provides a path back to the electrical source if a live wire touches an appliance frame or other metal part. That path helps a breaker or fuse shut off the fault.</p></div>
        </div>
        <p>For example, a damaged wire can energize a washing machine's metal case. The equipment grounding connection helps the protective device clear that fault. We don't rely on current traveling through the soil to trip the breaker.</p>

        <h2>What we check</h2>
        <ul><li><strong>The grounding electrodes and connections.</strong> We look for damage, corrosion, loose clamps, and changes to the system.</li><li><strong>Metal water piping and other required bonding.</strong> Plumbing changes can interrupt existing connections.</li><li><strong>The panel and equipment connections.</strong> We check where neutral and grounding connections belong for that installation.</li><li><strong>Added equipment.</strong> A generator, spa, hot tub, or new circuit can need particular grounding and bonding arrangements.</li></ul>
        <p>We'll explain any problem we find and the work to correct it. The equipment, existing installation, and applicable local requirements determine the details.</p>

        <h2>Common things that need a closer look</h2>
        <h3>Plumbing has changed</h3>
        <p>A plastic replacement pipe can change a path that an older electrical system relied on. We check the whole grounding arrangement instead of assuming the old clamp still does its job.</p>
        <h3>An inspection mentions missing bonding</h3>
        <p>Send us the wording from the inspection report. We'll check what it refers to and explain the correction. You don't need to interpret the electrical terminology first.</p>
        <h3>You're adding a generator</h3>
        <p>The generator's neutral arrangement and transfer equipment determine the grounding and bonding setup. An extra ground rod is not a universal fix. We check the system before connecting it.</p>
        <h3>You're installing a hot tub or spa</h3>
        <p>Water and nearby metal make the details especially important. We check the equipment instructions and the installation requirements before connecting power.</p>

        <h2>What affects the cost?</h2>
        <p>We need to see what already exists, which connections need work, and how we can reach them. If you're replacing a panel, we include the grounding and bonding work that the upgrade requires in the scope.</p>
        <p><a href="/electrical-panel-upgrade.html">Read about panel upgrades →</a></p>
        <p><a class="btn btn-primary" href="/get-help.html">Ask us to check your system →</a></p>

        <div class="content-callout warning-box"><h2>Don't ignore a shock or tingling sensation</h2><p>Stop using the affected equipment and keep others away. Don't test it by touching it again. Call an electrician; a small shock can signal a serious fault. Keep panel and equipment covers in place.</p><a href="tel:7342169437">Call (734) 216-9437</a></div>
    </div></div></section>
